        "title": "Computing Second Order Derivatives Using Leibnitz Notation"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "690274a2-8a45-50eb-9fc2-1e4aa16bd1d2",
        ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $y=2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right).$\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-4{x}^{-1/2}\\right)) \\\\  & =2\\left(8\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{7}}{4}+1-(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 4{x}^{-3/2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\left(2{x}^{7}+1+2{x}^{-3/2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\left(2{x}^{7}+1+2{x}^{-3/2}\\right)) \\\\  & =2\\left(7\\cdot 2{x}^{6}+0+(-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot 2{x}^{-5/2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\left(14{x}^{6}-3{x}^{-5/2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\left(14{x}^{6}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
